  # (0.3.1 C5) The CROSS-EMIT row: TEKO_TARGET names a target this host may be able to BUILD but
  # cannot EXECUTE. R3 says emit normally; C5 says report "emitted, not executed" and skip the run
  # instead of failing on an exec of a foreign-format binary. The RUN is honest-skipped by
  # cross_note_for_name (the same classification the build's own report uses) while `Then object
  # well-formed` still asserts the emitted object IS the requested format — which is the whole point
  # of a cross build. On a host with no cross toolchain the row skips earlier, at
  # target_toolchain_skip_reason.
  # `When built`, not `When built and run` — the row claims the OBJECT, and only the object. Owner
  # ruling 2026-07-28: *"teria que fazer asserção de erro/pânico, se sabemos que vai ocorrer, só
  # inverter para passar e deixar registrado."*
  #
  # It used to declare `Then exit = 42`, which no host that cannot exec a foreign-format binary was
  # ever going to check — so the runner honestly SKIPPED it and `no_skips_gate.sh` failed the lane.
  # The skip was correct: a claim really was left outstanding. Dropping the claim the row cannot
  # keep is what makes it honest, not louder.
  #
  # `Then object well-formed` still runs, and it is the whole point of a cross build: the emitted
  # artefact IS the requested format. PROMOTE BACK to `When built and run` + `Then exit = 42` the
  # day this lane gains an emulator — at which point the exit becomes checkable and the row should
  # go back to checking it.
  Scenario: own_cross_x86_64_windows_emits_coff (0.3.1 C5 — the object is the claim)
    Given target = "x86_64-windows"
    Given source = "cases/cross_exit42.tks"
    When built
    Then object well-formed

  # (AArch64-ELF crumb 3) The arm64/Linux-ELF target, which until now DID NOT EXIST: `NativeTarget`
  # carried `Arm64Macho` and `X8664Linux` but no `Arm64Linux`, so `host_target_for_os("linux",
  # "arm64")` honestly REFUSED — and every own-native row on the arm64-Linux leg honest-skipped
  # behind that refusal while the leg reported green. The refusal was correct code for a target that
  # did not exist; the fix was to make the target exist.
  #
  # `When built`, not `When built and run`, for the SAME reason the COFF row above gives: on an
  # x86_64 host `arm64-linux` is CROSS, the run is honest-skipped, and a declared `Then exit` would
  # be a claim no host here can keep — which is a skip, and `no_skips_gate.sh` is right to fail a
  # lane over it. The claim this row makes is the OBJECT: a well-formed, linker-consumable AArch64
  # `ET_REL` carrying e_machine 183.
  #
  # AND IT IS THE ROW THAT NEEDED check_elf.sh's SECOND ARGUMENT. Before that argument existed the
  # checker derived its expected `e_machine` from `uname -m`, so this row's perfectly correct
  # AArch64 bytes would have been rejected on an x86_64 host with "not an X86-64 object" — a false
  # red produced by asking the host a question only the TARGET can answer. Measured, both
  # directions: with the argument, an AArch64 object passes as `arm64-linux` and FAILS as
  # `x86_64-linux`; the mirror case (x86_64 bytes claimed as `arm64-linux`) fails too.
  #
  # The exit code lives on `own_host_os_arch_exit210_runs` below, where a host can actually check it.
  Scenario: own_cross_arm64_linux_emits_elf (AArch64-ELF — the object is the claim)
    Given target = "arm64-linux"
    Given source = "cases/cross_exit210.tks"
    When built
    Then object well-formed

  # (0.3.1 C5) The host os-arch spelled EXPLICITLY: the reproduced-good path of the motivating bug
  # (an explicit TEKO_TARGET made the whole vertical work, exit 42).
  #
  # IT NAMES `host-os-arch`, NOT `x86_64-linux` (owner ruling 2026-07-27: *"precisa ensinar o
  # regressor a entender diferentes OSes e arquiteturas para variar um mesmo teste"*). The subject of
  # this row is that a target spelled OUT works — not that the target is x86_64. Hardcoding the
  # architecture made it a CROSS row on every host that is not x86_64-linux, where it honest-skipped
  # instead of running: the same test, deleted by a constant. The token resolves to whatever os-arch
  # this host natively builds, so the row RUNS on every host and still exercises the
  # explicitly-spelled path (which the plain `host` sentinel, exporting no `TEKO_TARGET` at all, does
  # not). The rows above keep their explicit `x86_64-windows` / `wasm32-wasi` names because for THEM
  # the architecture IS the subject, and an honest skip without the cross toolchain is correct.
  Scenario: own_explicit_host_os_arch_runs (0.3.1 C5 — the reproduced-good path)
    Given target = "host-os-arch"
    Given source = "cases/cross_exit42.tks"
    When built and run
    Then exit = 42
    Then object well-formed

  # (AArch64-ELF crumb 3) THE VALUE THE arm64-Linux LANE OWES, and the only row that can collect it:
  # `host-os-arch` resolves to whatever os-arch this host natively builds, so this row RUNS on every
  # host — and on an arm64 Linux runner that name is now `arm64-linux`, which means the whole
  # vertical is exercised end to end and JUDGED BY ITS VALUE: arm64 isel → AAPCS64 regalloc → arm64
  # encode → `emit_elf_arm64` → the host `cc` links the ELF → the binary runs → exit 210. Before
  # `Arm64Linux` existed, `tkr_resolve_target_name` degraded `host-os-arch` to the plain `host`
  # sentinel there (the resolver had no name to spell out) and the row honest-skipped.
  #
  # `Then object well-formed` collects the second half on the same build: the emitted object's
  # `e_machine` must be the one the RESOLVED os-arch names — AArch64 on the arm64 leg, X86-64 on the
  # x86_64 leg — which is the assertion check_elf.sh's second argument makes possible.
  #
  # 210 is this lane's own exit value, distinct from the corpus-wide 42, so a row asserting it can
  # never be satisfied by another fixture's binary being run by mistake.
  Scenario: own_host_os_arch_exit210_runs (AArch64-ELF — the host vertical, judged by its value)
    Given target = "host-os-arch"
    Given source = "cases/cross_exit210.tks"
    When built and run
    Then exit = 210
    Then object well-formed


