Feature: str query/slice builtins — `contains`/`ends_with`/`slice`/`slice_to`/`slice_from` have a native lowering

  # (0.3.1.0 degrau 11 / raiz-comum §R5) `call_symbol` had no resolution for any of these five bare
  # names, so a reached call fell through `unresolved_builtin_stop` and honest-stopped ("builtin
  # `contains` not yet lowered") — first surfaced through `teko::assert::str_contains`'s own body
  # (`src/assert/assert.tks`), which every `.tkt` test file in this project calls via the injected
  # assert seed. `native_builtin_symbol` now resolves all five, split by RETURN SHAPE:
  # `contains`/`ends_with` are `(str, str) -> bool` (`builtin_str_query_symbol`) and need no
  # out-parameter-length twin — a `bool` fits the single result register every scalar call already
  # captures. `slice`/`slice_to`/`slice_from` are `str`-returning (`builtin_str_slice_symbol`) and
  # resolve to the runtime's out-parameter-length twins (`tk_str_slice_len`/`tk_str_slice_to_len`/
  # `tk_str_slice_from_len`, `teko_rt.h`) through the SAME `lower_len_out_call` shape 0.3.1.0
  # degrau 9 built for `tk_str_concat_len` — `lower_call_fat`'s own generic tail already appends
  # the hidden length out-slot once the symbol resolves, so no new lowering ARM was needed.
  #
  # The two rows are MEMBERS of this channel (one build, no extra cost): one exercises the
  # predicate pair (a match, a miss, and the empty-needle/empty-suffix edge each runtime function
  # documents as trivially true), the other exercises all three slice builtins by both LENGTH and
  # CONTENT, so a plausible length with the wrong bytes is caught too.
  Scenario: str_contains_and_ends_with (own-native — the two `str` predicate builtins)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario str_contains_and_ends_with: ok"

  Scenario: str_slice_family (own-native — `slice`/`slice_to`/`slice_from`, each length AND content)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario str_slice_family: ok"


Feature: a breakless loop tail — a value-returning function/arm whose LAST statement is a bare `loop { … }` with no `break` at all

  # (0.3.1.0 degrau 13) `teko::backend::idom_intersect` (`src/backend/stackify.tks`) stopped the
  # compiler's own native self-build: "if-expression arm's tail statement does not yield a value
  # (internal)". `lower_tail_non_expr` (`src/lir/lower.tks`) treated ANY non-`return`-terminated
  # tail statement as an internal checker-invariant break, but `lower_loop` always reports
  # `terminated=false` for a loop (break or not) — and the checker itself makes NO claim about a
  # non-expression tail (`check_trailing_value`'s own doc, typer.tks), TRUSTING that every
  # reachable path diverges, exactly the trust the C emitter already honors via a bare
  # `__builtin_unreachable()` after such a tail (`cg_stmt_c_terminates` + `codegen.tks:9008`). The
  # message's own "(internal)" framing was itself the bug: not a broken invariant, a genuine
  # program shape the lowering had not implemented yet. Fixed by reusing the ALREADY-EXISTING
  # diverging-tail defensive-constant treatment (`diverging_tail_value`, built for a diverging
  # `panic`/`exit` tail expression) for this one more statement shape — SAFER than the C route's
  # raw UB (a well-defined placeholder, never observed under the checker's own guarantee) and
  # zero new machinery.
  #
  # The row mirrors `idom_intersect`'s own shape EXACTLY (`gcd_subtract`, `corpus.tks`): the
  # function's last statement is a bare breakless `loop`, wrapping two NESTED loops that each DO
  # `break`, one per "finger" walked toward the other — checked by VALUE across three GCD pairs,
  # including the x==y edge where neither inner loop ever runs.
  Scenario: loop_tail_no_break (own-native — a value-returning function/arm whose tail is a breakless `loop`)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario loop_tail_no_break: ok"


Feature: a FAT breakless loop tail — a `str`-returning function/arm whose LAST statement is a bare `loop { … }` with no `break` at all

  # (0.3.1.0 degrau 20) `src/build/regression.tks::snippet_chunk_skip_attr_lines` stopped the
  # compiler's own native self-build with the SAME message degrau 13 already closed once:
  # "if-expression arm's tail statement does not yield a value (internal)". Degrau 13 fixed only
  # the SCALAR axis (`lower_tail_non_expr`, falling back to `diverging_tail_value`); its FAT
  # (`str`/`[]T`) counterpart, `lower_tail_non_expr_fat`, honest-stopped unconditionally — a
  # separate, still-open sibling gap the closed-set doc on `lower_tail_expr_stmt_fat` named
  # explicitly ("no fixture in this closure needs a diverging fat tail"). The message's own
  # "(internal)" framing was, again, not true: the checker's own trust in a non-expression tail
  # (`check_trailing_value`, typer.tks) draws no distinction between a scalar and a fat return
  # type, so a fat-typed function ending in a breakless loop is exactly as legitimate as
  # `idom_intersect`'s scalar one. Fixed by `diverging_tail_value_fat`, the fat counterpart of
  # `diverging_tail_value` — two defensive zero constants (ptr, len) instead of one, never
  # observed under the same checker guarantee.
  #
  # The row mirrors `snippet_chunk_skip_attr_lines`'s own shape (`strip_leading_hash_lines`,
  # corpus.tks): the function's last statement is a bare breakless `loop`, its two `return`s the
  # only exits, wrapped around an inner loop that DOES `break` — checked by VALUE across no
  # leading `#` line, one, two consecutive ones, an all-`#` input with no trailing newline (the
  # `return ""` exit), and the empty string (the outer loop's FIRST iteration already exits).
  Scenario: fat_loop_tail_no_break (own-native — a str-returning function/arm whose tail is a breakless `loop`)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario fat_loop_tail_no_break: ok"


Feature: the built-in `error`'s six-field native layout, zeroed, plus `err_loc`/`err_typed`

  # (0.3.1.0 degrau 22) `error_struct_layout` registered ONE field (`message`); the checker reads
  # six (`message`/`file`/`line`/`col`/`expected`/`actual`), so a program reading `.line` honest-
  # stopped natively ("unknown field `line` on struct `error`"), starting with the compiler's own
  # `const_type_located`. The layout now matches the C route's `tk_error` field for field, in the
  # SAME commit that zeroes the five fields no Teko-surface literal writes (`zero_error_
  # diagnostics`) — growing the slot without zeroing it would have left 56 bytes of frame garbage
  # readable as a plausible-looking `line`/`col`, a silent divergence rather than a stop.
  #
  # The row is a MEMBER of this channel (one build, no extra cost) and asserts every field by
  # VALUE, plus the variant-wrapped arm proving the grown 72-byte payload still boxes by ADDRESS
  # into the pre-existing 24-byte uniform wrapper (degrau 15/21) without growing it.
  Scenario: error_diagnostic_fields (own-native — the six built-in `error` fields, zeroed where unwritten)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario error_diagnostic_fields: ok"


Feature: `err_loc`/`err_typed` — the `error` diagnostic-adornment builtins, native

  # (0.3.1.0 degrau 22) `tk_error_loc`/`tk_error_types` (`teko_rt.h`) are `static inline` with no
  # linkable symbol, so this backend lowers `teko::error::err_loc`/`err_typed` directly in
  # `lower_call` (the SAME molde `last_index_of`/`str_from_utf8` already use), through a shared
  # `copy_error_value`: a fresh slot, `tk_mem_copy`'d from the source, then the two named fields
  # overwritten on the COPY. A mutation in place would have been silent and wrong — this backend
  # represents an `error` VALUE by the address of its slot, so adorning without copying would
  # mutate the caller's own binding, diverging from the C route's copy-return semantics with no
  # stop at all.
  #
  # `err_typed` had NO prior measurement in this lane despite more than double `err_loc`'s own
  # call count, and its own distinct, confirmed native stop ("builtin `err_typed` not yet lowered
  # (N2)").
  #
  # Each row is a MEMBER of this channel (one build, no extra cost) and asserts the adorned
  # fields by VALUE AND that the source was NOT mutated; the second row chains both builtins (the
  # ONE site in the compiler where they compose, `surface_at`).
  Scenario: err_loc (own-native — a copy of an `error` with `line`/`col` overwritten, source untouched)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario err_loc: ok"

  Scenario: err_typed (own-native — `err_loc`'s fat-field twin, chained through `err_loc` itself)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario err_typed: ok"


