Feature: float-to-text — `ftoa`/`f64_g17` lower to the runtime's own `%.17g` renderer, by VALUE

  # (0.3.1.0 degrau 27) `teko::ftoa(f64) -> str` had NO lowering in the own backend, and it is the
  # renderer `teko::codegen::cb_f64_literal` spells every float literal of the C route's output
  # through — so the native SELF-BUILD of the compiler stopped there and no native gen2 existed at
  # all. Both float-to-text names (`ftoa` and `f64_g17` — one `%.17g` renderer, two spellings) now
  # resolve to their own out-parameter-length twins, the same molde the integer half has used since
  # degrau 9.
  #
  # Every row asserts TEXT, never "it compiled": a renderer that emitted garbage would pass a
  # compilation fixture. The expected strings are the ones the C ROUTE prints for the same program.
  Scenario: ftoa_runtime_text (own-native — five RUNTIME values, `+0.0`/`-0.0`/`1.0`/`-2.5`/`1/3`, each pinned to its `%.17g` text)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario ftoa_runtime_text: ok"

  # The LITERAL direction is a separate row because it travels a separate road: a float literal is a
  # `LConstFloat` BIT PATTERN on the own backend and a SPELLED C constant on the C route. Two defects
  # lived in that spelling and only a value comparison finds them — `-0.0` lost its sign (`%.17g`
  # renders it `-0`, which C reads as the `int` zero) and a literal that overflows binary64
  # (`1.0e400`) did not COMPILE at all (`error: 'inf' undeclared`).
  Scenario: ftoa_literal_text (own-native — the literal road, including `-0.0` and the overflowing `1.0e400`)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario ftoa_literal_text: ok"

  # `inf`/`-inf`/NaN ARE representable on this surface, so they are covered rather than skipped. The
  # infinities are pinned exactly; the NaN is pinned by SUFFIX only, because its sign and payload are
  # the host libc's business (glibc prints `-nan` for `inf - inf`) and this corpus runs on glibc AND
  # on musl.
  Scenario: ftoa_nonfinite_text (own-native — `inf`, `-inf` and a NaN, produced by runtime arithmetic)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario ftoa_nonfinite_text: ok"

  # `f64_g17` reaches a DIFFERENT runtime symbol (`tk_f64_g17_len`) than `ftoa` does, so comparing the
  # two on identical values is evidence rather than a restated expectation.
  Scenario: f64_g17_agrees_with_ftoa (own-native — the second `%.17g` spelling, cross-checked against the first)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario f64_g17_agrees_with_ftoa: ok"

  # A no-format-spec float hole (`$"{x}"`) used to be its own honest stop even though the C route
  # renders it through this exact builtin. The sibling is closed through the same twin, and each hole
  # is compared against a DIRECT `teko::ftoa` call on the same value — two lowering paths, one symbol.
  Scenario: float_hole_natural_form (own-native — `$"{x}"` over a float, against its direct-call twin)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario float_hole_natural_form: ok"

  # THE DEFECT THIS DEGRAU'S SIBLING SEARCH FOUND, green since degrau 19: every `tk_*` renderer
  # declares a `double` parameter while an `f32` hole physically holds single precision, and nothing
  # widened it — the C route gets the conversion from `cc`'s implicit `(double)` and the own backend
  # got nothing. Measured against the C route on the same program: `$"{f:F2}"` over `f: f32 = 2.5`
  # printed `2.50` through C and `0.10` through the own backend; `$"{f:G}"` printed `2.5` and
  # `4.65274e-310`. A wrong VALUE with no diagnostic. The two constants are a matched pair — `2.5` is
  # exactly representable in both widths, `0.1` is not — so a route that renders one right and the
  # other wrong is reading the register at the wrong width.
  Scenario: f32_text_is_its_own_value (own-native — an `f32` through all four text routes, direct, natural, static-spec and dynamic-spec)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario f32_text_is_its_own_value: ok"

Feature: slice/str element READ — `recv[i]` is bounds-checked on the native backend too (0.3.1.0 leitura-fora-de-fronteira)

  # Degrau 28 closed the element WRITE and, in closing it, MEASURED that the element READ had no guard
  # at all. Reproduced on this compiler before any change, same source, both routes:
  #
  #     xs[5] on a 3-element slice
  #       own-native : prints `read=0` and exits 0        <- garbage, silently
  #       C route    : 2:9: teko: deliberate panic: index out of bounds
  #
  # Under the oracle rule a native/C divergence is a NATIVE bug until proven otherwise, and the C route
  # had bounds-checked both `str[i]` and `[]T[i]` from the start (`codegen::emit_index`, a stmt-expr that
  # single-evaluates the index and compares it against `.len`). So the native read was a silent WRONG
  # VALUE — the most dangerous class this lane hunts. The aggregate-element arm was worse than garbage:
  # it SEGFAULTED (rc=139), because the load walked a stored element pointer out of the buffer.
  #
  # All four element shapes were measured to diverge and now agree: SCALAR, FAT (`[]str`, `[][]T`),
  # AGGREGATE (held by address) and `str[i]` — plus CONSTANT, DYNAMIC and NEGATIVE indices. The guard is
  # the very one the write uses (`lower_index_bounds_guard`), the index is lowered EXACTLY ONCE and
  # shared by the guard and the address, and the trap calls the SAME positioned `tk_panic_oob_at` the C
  # route's own read guard calls — measured identical, position included.
  #
  # The CONSTANT index still folds its whole byte offset into one `field_addr`: the guard needs the index
  # in a register, not in the address. So the read pays a compare and a branch, and nothing else.
  #
  # Every row below is a MEMBER of this channel (one build, no extra cost) and asserts the VALUES an
  # in-bounds read gives back, because that is what proves the guard did not break the good path — the
  # LAST index of every container is read on purpose, since a guard written `<=` walks one element past
  # the buffer and one written `< len - 1` rejects the last element of every correct program.
  Scenario: slice_elem_read_boundaries (own-native — index 0, a middle index and the LAST, constant and dynamic, over an array literal's buffer and a push-grown one)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario slice_elem_read_boundaries: ok"

  Scenario: slice_elem_read_widths (own-native — i32/u8/byte/bool/f64 and an enum carrier, each read back at its own width with a neighbour re-checked)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario slice_elem_read_widths: ok"

  Scenario: slice_elem_read_fat (own-native — a `[]str` and a `[][]i64` element by LENGTH and CONTENT; the arm whose receiver is now lowered as a fat producer, for the length half the guard needs)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario slice_elem_read_fat: ok"

  Scenario: slice_elem_read_nested (own-native — `nn[i][j]`, the guarded fat read chained: the outer guard runs against a length the inner guarded read produced)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario slice_elem_read_nested: ok"

  Scenario: str_index_in_bounds (own-native — `str[i]` at 0, middle and last byte, over a literal, a concatenation and a multi-byte string; `lower_index_str`, the other guarded read path)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario str_index_in_bounds: ok"

  # The BOUNDS side, and each scenario needs its OWN file for the reason the cast-guard and store-guard
  # rows already record: a panic aborts the process and would take every later assertion of the channel's
  # one program with it. `Then it traps` + `stderr pattern` rather than an exit code, because an abort's
  # status is synthesised by whoever ran the child — no number distinguishes "the guard fired" from "the
  # program crashed", while the pattern names WHICH guard killed it.
  #
  # Every row runs on BOTH routes, and that pairing IS the oracle. Measured, before and after, on the
  # same compiler: the C route panicked at `2:9`/`3:9` in every one of them, and the own-native rows now
  # print the identical line, column and message.
  Scenario: slice_read_oob_panics (own-native — a constant read one past the last index)
    Given source = "cases/slice_read_oob_panics.tks"
    When built and run
    Then it traps
    And stderr pattern = "teko: deliberate panic: index out of bounds"

  Scenario: slice_read_oob_panics (C route — the route that was already right)
    Given source = "cases/slice_read_oob_panics.tks"
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When built and run
    Then it traps
    And stderr pattern = "teko: deliberate panic: index out of bounds"

  Scenario: slice_read_negative_index_panics (own-native — a negative index is the huge unsigned value it already is in the carrier, so the SAME unsigned compare rejects it)
    Given source = "cases/slice_read_negative_index_panics.tks"
    When built and run
    Then it traps
    And stderr pattern = "teko: deliberate panic: index out of bounds"

  Scenario: slice_read_negative_index_panics (C route)
    Given source = "cases/slice_read_negative_index_panics.tks"
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
